README.md
    1→# k(r)ep - A high-performance string search utility
    6→`krep` is an optimized string search utility designed for maximum throughput and efficiency when processing large files and directories. It is built with performance in mind, offering multiple search algorithms and SIMD acceleration when available.
    8→> **Note:**  
    9→> Krep is not intended to be a full replacement or direct competitor to feature-rich tools like `grep` or `ripgrep`. Instead, it aims to be a minimal, efficient, and pragmatic tool focused on speed and simplicity.
    10→>
    11→> Krep provides the essential features needed for fast searching, without the extensive options and complexity of more comprehensive search utilities. Its design philosophy is to deliver the fastest possible search for the most common use cases, with a clean and minimal interface.
    13→## The Story Behind the Name
    …
    15→The name "krep" has an interesting origin. It is inspired by the Icelandic word "kreppan," which means "to grasp quickly" or "to catch firmly." I came across this word while researching efficient techniques for pattern recognition.
    17→Just as skilled fishers identify patterns in the water to locate fish quickly, I designed "krep" to find patterns in text with maximum efficiency. The name is also short and easy to remember—perfect for a command-line utility that users might type hundreds of times per day.
    19→## Key Features
    …
    21→- **Multiple search algorithms**: Boyer-Moore-Horspool, KMP, Aho-Corasick for optimal performance across different pattern types
    22→- **Algorithm selection**: Automatic smart selection with optional `--algo` override for fine-tuning
    23→- **SIMD acceleration**: Uses SSE4.2, AVX2, or NEON instructions when available for blazing-fast searches
    24→- **Memory-mapped I/O**: Maximizes throughput when processing large files
    25→- **Multi-threaded search**: Automatically parallelizes searches across available CPU cores
    26→- **Regex support**: POSIX Extended Regular Expression searching
    27→- **Multiple pattern search**: Efficiently search for multiple patterns simultaneously using Aho-Corasick
    28→- **Recursive directory search**: Skip binary files and common non-code directories
    29→- **Gitignore support**: Respect `.gitignore` files during recursive search with `--gitignore`
    30→- **Stdin pattern input**: Read patterns from stdin with `-f -` for seamless pipeline integration
    31→- **Colored output**: Highlights matches for better readability
    32→- **UI/UX refresh (v2.2)**: New terminal color palette, clearer `-o` line index styling, and a redesigned help screen
    33→- **Specialized algorithms**: Optimized handling for single-character and short patterns
    34→- **Match Limiting**: Stop searching a file after a specific number of matching lines are found.
    36→## What's New in v2.2.0
    …
    38→- Refined terminal-first UI with a cleaner, more legible color theme
    39→- Better visual hierarchy in `-o` mode (filename, line index, match highlight)
    40→- Improved `--help` layout with grouped sections and clearer scanning
    42→## Installation
    …
    44→### Using Homebrew (macOS)
    …
    46→If you are on macOS and have Homebrew installed, you can install `krep` easily:
    48→```bash
    49→brew install krep
    50→```
    52→### Building from Source
    …
    54→```bash
    55→# Clone the repository
    56→git clone https://github.com/davidesantangelo/krep.git
    57→cd krep
    59→# Build and install
    60→make
    61→sudo make install
    63→# uninstall
    64→sudo make uninstall
    65→```
    67→The binary will be installed to `/usr/local/bin/krep` by default.
    69→### Requirements
    …
    71→- GCC or compatible C compiler
    72→- POSIX-compliant system (Linux, macOS, BSD)
    73→- pthread support
    75→### Build Options
    …
    77→Override default optimization settings in the Makefile:
    79→```bash
    80→# Disable architecture-specific optimizations
    81→make ENABLE_ARCH_DETECTION=0
    82→```
    84→## Usage
    …
    86→```bash
    87→krep [OPTIONS] PATTERN [FILE | DIRECTORY]
    88→krep [OPTIONS] -e PATTERN [FILE | DIRECTORY]
    89→krep [OPTIONS] -f FILE [FILE | DIRECTORY]
    90→krep [OPTIONS] -s PATTERN STRING_TO_SEARCH
    91→krep [OPTIONS] PATTERN < FILE
    92→cat FILE | krep [OPTIONS] PATTERN
    93→echo 'pattern' | krep -f - [FILE | DIRECTORY]
    94→```
    96→## Usage Examples
    …
    98→Search for a fixed string in a file:
    100→```bash
    101→krep -F "value: 100%" config.ini
    102→```
    104→Search recursively:
    106→```bash
    107→krep -r "function" ./project
    108→```
    110→Search recursively respecting `.gitignore`:
    112→```bash
    113→krep -r --gitignore "TODO" ./project
    114→```
    116→Read patterns from stdin (pipe-friendly):
    118→```bash
    119→echo 'pattern' | krep -f - target.txt
    120→```
    122→Whole word search (matches only complete words):
    124→```bash
    125→krep -w 'cat' samples/text.en
    126→```
    128→Use with piped input:
    130→```bash
    131→cat krep.c | krep 'c'
    132→```
    134→## Command Line Options
    …
    136→- `-i, --ignore-case` Case-insensitive search
    137→- `-c, --count` Count matching lines only
    138→- `-o, --only-matching` Print only the matched parts of lines
    139→- `-e PATTERN, --pattern=PATTERN` Specify pattern(s). Can be used multiple times.
    140→- `-f FILE, --file=FILE` Read patterns from FILE, one per line. Use `-` for stdin.
    141→- `-m NUM, --max-count=NUM` Stop searching each file after finding NUM matching lines.
    142→- `-E, --extended-regexp` Use POSIX Extended Regular Expressions
    143→- `-F, --fixed-strings` Interpret pattern as fixed string(s) (default unless -E is used)
    144→- `-r, --recursive` Recursively search directories
    145→- `--gitignore` Respect `.gitignore` files during recursive search
    146→- `--algo=ALGO` Force search algorithm: `auto` (default), `bm` (Boyer-Moore), `kmp` (KMP)
    147→- `-t NUM, --threads=NUM` Use NUM threads for file search (default: auto)
    148→- `-s STRING, --string=STRING` Search in the provided STRING instead of file(s)
    149→- `-w, --word-regexp` Match only whole words
    150→- `--color[=WHEN]` Control color output ('always', 'never', 'auto')
    151→- `--no-simd` Explicitly disable SIMD acceleration
    152→- `-v, --version` Show version information
    153→- `-h, --help` Show help message
    155→## Performance Benchmarks
    …
    157→Benchmarks are run with the official dataset:
    159→```bash
    160→curl -LO 'https://burntsushi.net/stuff/subtitles2016-sample.en.gz'
    161→gzip -dk subtitles2016-sample.en.gz
    162→```
    164→You can reproduce the `krep` vs `ripgrep` comparison with:
    166→```bash
    167→make bench-rg
    168→# optional: RUNS=7 bash test/benchmark_krep_vs_rg.sh Sherlock
    169→```
    171→### krep v2.1.0 vs ripgrep (warm cache, 7 runs average baseline)
    …
    173→| Pattern | krep avg real (s) | ripgrep avg real (s) | Speedup |
    174→| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
    175→| `the` | 0.175714 | 0.330000 | 1.88x |
    176→| `Sherlock` | 0.041429 | 0.080000 | 1.93x |
    178→_Measured on macOS ARM64 with `test/benchmark_krep_vs_rg.sh`. Results vary by CPU, storage and cache state._
    180→## How Krep Works
    …
    182→Krep achieves its high performance through several key techniques:
    184→### 1. Smart Algorithm Selection
    …
    186→Krep automatically selects the optimal search algorithm based on the pattern and available hardware:
    188→- **Boyer-Moore-Horspool** for most literal string searches
    189→- **Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP)** for very short patterns and repetitive patterns
    190→- **memchr optimization** for single-character patterns
    191→- **SIMD Acceleration** (SSE4.2, AVX2, or NEON) for compatible hardware
    192→- **Regex Engine** for regular expression patterns
    193→- **Aho-Corasick** for efficient multiple pattern matching (auto-selected with multiple `-e` patterns)
    195→Use `--algo=bm` or `--algo=kmp` to override the automatic selection for single-pattern literal searches.
    197→### 2. Multi-threading Architecture
    …
    199→Krep utilizes parallel processing to dramatically speed up searches:
    201→- Automatically detects available CPU cores
    202→- Divides large files into chunks for parallel processing
    203→- Implements thread pooling for maximum efficiency
    204→- Optimized thread count selection based on file size
    205→- Careful boundary handling to ensure no matches are missed
    207→### 3. Memory-Mapped I/O
    …
    209→Instead of traditional read operations:
    211→- Memory maps files for direct access by the CPU
    212→- Significantly reduces I/O overhead
    213→- Enables CPU cache optimization
    214→- Progressive prefetching for larger files
    216→### 4. Optimized Data Structures
    …
    218→- Zero-copy architecture where possible
    219→- Efficient match position tracking
    220→- Lock-free aggregation of results
    222→### 5. Skipping Non-Relevant Content
    …
    224→When using recursive search (`-r`), Krep automatically:
    226→- Skips common binary file types
    227→- Ignores version control directories (`.git`, `.svn`)
    228→- Bypasses dependency directories (`node_modules`, `venv`)
    229→- Detects binary content to avoid searching non-text files
